i think in a funny way i didn't i didn't  find a film i think the film found me  i i made a lot of short films i started  making films at a very young age i i  made my first shot at 19  and  um after making a lot of shorts  i went to film school twice  in 2010 i i knew i've made a lot of  shots it's time to make my first feature  film  i'm not like some directors that you  know they've got lots of ideas in their  head and they would just pitch like 10  ideas and producers to go like yeah  let's do that you know for me you know i  need to be obsessed by something  how did the obsession begin  there was there was a period of time  when a lot of childhood memories just  came  flooding back into my head i think in  singapore you spend most of your time  sort of like growing up chasing grades  becoming an adult you know starting a  career and that  your childhood sort of gets swept  underneath the carpet you know i think  it's been repressed for so long  and all these memories just came back  and and i remember you know that we had  this filipino woman you know um who  worked with us for eight years she came  when i was four  she left when i was 12. um  her name is  um so it really started from there i  think that that one  it really started from one emotion when  i was 12 and  we were sending her off at the airport  and i remember  i was just crying and crying and crying  and it was so painful  and it was that recollection that really  made me ask a lot of questions like how  is it that a child can be so attached to  someone  you know who's who's not part of who you  are you know she she's not family she's  a stranger she's a servant um you know  yeah you're not connected by blood so it  it really started from there  is it common for uh filipino nannies to  uh raise children in middle to lower  middle class families  i think in in countries like singapore  or even like and hong kong and very  developed asian economies um it's a very  pragmatic  sort of thing to have to employ a mate  from  the philippines or from indonesia i  wouldn't say you know like in the west  having a mate is a very bozoir idea  usually it's a very middle class upper  class thing but but in singapore it's  very much because of economic reasons  because very pragmatic reasons you've  got a very huge middle class we have got  a lot of dual income families white  collar working parents and  for them to bring the bread and butter  someone has to look after the kids so it  was it's it's a very practical decision  to employ me it's not because the family  is ridiculously rich it's just because  you know  you just need to have someone the film  itself the script itself took two years  you know i don't actually find structure  i don't actually find  um setups and payoffs you know so easily  for me i have to go on the journey of  the characters  i slowly discover what i'm trying to say  you know um  what what is it that i wanna um i wanna  express in this film  can you tell us about the challenges you  faced in casting jolly i mean the the  heart of this film is this young boy had  he acted before how did you find it no  he was he has a complete fresh face  hasn't been in theater hasn't been on  film hasn't been on tv it hasn't even  been in the school play  i knew i wanted um because i'm very very  wary of actors  child actors that have gone through a  lot of training because i wanted  something very honest i wanted something  very organic i wanted something very  natural  so  we started a very long casting process  it was a very rigorous process we took  about 10 months  in those 10 months we went to about 20  schools in singapore we saw about 8 000  children  out of the 8 000 children we saw  we formally auditioned about 2000 of  them  and then  we shortlisted 150  and i spent about six months  uh every saturday and sunday doing  workshops with these kids  so it was it was like a reality show  where  where we were eliminating like 150 down  to six months later we have like the top  ten what scene did they  it wasn't about scenes for me it's about  finding out who these people really are  and i think a lot of times there might  be kids that sparkle on a day  but but if you're gonna be holding a  film if you're gonna be filming for like  25-30 days you need someone who's  consistent with someone who who has real  presence  um and i wanted to see if if if the kids  could deliver that  this boy and his real name is jala  you know i actually renamed the  character of the of the boy after i  found him because tiana in chinese chia  means family and love means happiness  and when i was when when i when i casted  him i was thinking  wow that's great irony for this film  because  it's really about a family that's not  very happy  yes and the boy's name is like happy  family  i think most filmmakers like to like to  go for  the cutest kid  the one with the smiley face the one  that  you know that you want to hug and kiss  and all that but he's not he's not the  prettiest of kids but there's something  about his face there's a  there's a fragility  and there's a fracture there and and  it's something you can't quite describe  you just know  something is not quite right with this  kid but you can't say what you know my  philosophy is that we're all trying you  know as people as humans we're all  trying to be the best of what we can  we all want to be  good workers good parents uh good  fathers good mothers we all try to be  the best but we can't because we're  imperfect and our flaws are underway you  know because there's gluttony and  there's greed and there's lust and all  of that so so if you look at my  characters  they're always trying  they're always trying but they'll never  get there and it's not really their  fault you know because because that's  what we are it's i think that's that's  when you have drama that's when you have  cinema it's because of our imperfections
 

